  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic energy-flow diagram for a recirculating accelerator or storage ring, showing how electrical and cryogenic power support the beam and where that energy ends up. A dashed loop at the top represents the recirculating beam path, with a beam injected into the machine and later sent to a beam dump. Inside the main pink beam channel, the central region is marked as RF available, indicating that radio-frequency systems replenish energy to the circulating beam.\n\nA key message is that the direct RF power needed for the beam can be relatively small, while the dominant power burden comes from cryogenics. The lower large pink block shows cryogenic power required to remove both static losses and especially dynamic heating, scaled as COP times the dynamic loss. This cooling demand is much larger than the beam-energy transfer itself. On the right, the extracted heat is shown as a blue block, emphasizing that most of the consumed wall-plug power ultimately becomes heat. A small blue arrow for synchrotron radiation indicates beam energy lost as radiation, and a thin branch labeled dynamic loss links RF/beam operation to the cryogenic load. Overall, the diagram stresses that cooling overhead dominates machine power consumption.",
